### 16.4 Governance Under Constraint: The Legal Vacuum

Chapter 3.3 and Chapter 6.4 already established that current space governance lacks the monitoring and sanctioning components of Ostrom's design principles. This chapter extends that finding: the vacuum is not merely technical but actively contested at the level of foundational law. At least three governance frameworks are directly relevant to space resource extraction — the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, the non-binding Artemis Accords, and the binding but thinly ratified 1979 Moon Agreement, which has only seventeen state parties. [springer](https://link.springer.com/978-981-95-0151-9) The Artemis Accords, now the dominant practical framework, remain structurally unresolved on the point Ostrom's principles identify as most critical: the Accords affirm resource extraction as legal while explicitly declining to characterize outer space as a global commons, and their "safety zone" provisions — while framed as non-appropriation measures — risk functioning as de facto exclusion zones around resource-rich terrain, with critics arguing the practical difference from territorial claims may be minimal. [New Space Economy](https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/05/a-serious-look-at-generation-ships-what-why-when/)

This is directly the Ostrom principle-4/5 failure mode identified in Chapter 6: monitoring and graduated sanctions are absent, and what exists instead is a coalition-based framework that has been characterized by critics as normatively centered on a single dominant actor while presenting the appearance of multilateral coalition-building, having bypassed the UN's consensus-based treaty process. [Diverse Daily](https://diversedaily.com/generation-ships-exploring-the-concept-and-challenges-of-interstellar-travel/) A Civilization Guardian theory of space settlement cannot treat this as background noise. It is the single largest institutional vulnerability the theory's own Chapter 6 framework predicts should produce failure.

### 16.5 The Hardest Case: Generation Ships and Total Institutional Isolation

Space settlement within the solar system at least preserves communication with Earth, however delayed. Interstellar settlement — the generation ship — removes even that. This is the theoretical limiting case for every Guardian principle simultaneously, and the existing literature on generation-ship design treats institutional collapse, not technical failure, as the primary risk. Historical examples of stable, long-lived institutions — religious orders, monarchies, democratic republics — may offer partial models, but none has operated under conditions remotely similar to a fully isolated, closed-ecology, multi-generational vessel. [Orbit Codex](https://orbitcodex.com/knowledge-base/generation-ships) The darkest documented proposal in the literature is instructive precisely because it violates nearly every Guardian principle at once: some analyses argue the ship's founding culture would require "heavy duty indoctrination and manipulation" to secure absolute mission dedication, potentially producing rigid, authoritarian governance in which the justice system itself would have to abandon incarceration or execution in favor of forced rehabilitation, since every crew member is an irreplaceable, mission-critical asset. [Grokipedia](https://grokipedia.com/page/Generation_ship)

This is precisely the concentration trajectory of Chapter 1.3 (Technology → Power → Concentration → Dogma → Suppression → Collapse) predicted from first principles, arrived at independently by generation-ship design theorists reasoning from resource constraints alone. It is strong indirect corroboration of the theory's core claim: absent deliberately engineered Guardian institutions (Freedom to Question, Chapter 5; Adaptive Capacity, Chapter 11), extreme resource constraint pushes governance toward exactly the authoritarian, dogmatic, error-suppressing attractor the theory identifies as civilizationally fatal — not because anyone chooses tyranny, but because it is the "obvious" local optimization under scarcity.

Even the physical substrate is unproven at the necessary scale: closed-loop biological life support has achieved full closure for at most about 180 days in the most successful controlled experiments, while the U.S. Biosphere 2 project ran into severe, near-fatal problems with oxygen depletion and crop failure well short of its intended two-year closure. [Project Hyperion](https://www.projecthyperion.org/) The Guardian theory's claim that material and human infrastructure are equally critical (Section 1.4) has no cleaner test case than this: a generation ship with perfect closed-loop biology and no Guardian-consistent governance is a well-engineered authoritarian trap; a generation ship with strong Guardian institutions and unreliable life support does not survive to test its governance at all.

### 16.6 Revised Statement of the Principle

«Space does not test whether humanity can build machines that keep people alive. It tests whether humanity can build institutions that keep people free, correctable, and cooperative once Earth is too far away to help.»

**Testable hypotheses:**
- H16.1: Settlements or analog missions with graduated, pre-planned autonomy transfer (following NASA's minimum-Earth-independence model) will show fewer Ch.14-style institutional-adaptation failures than settlements that seize full autonomy immediately upon technical feasibility.
- H16.2: Space governance frameworks embedding more of Ostrom's eight principles (monitoring, graduated sanctions, conflict resolution) will show measurably lower rates of resource-claim disputes than frameworks — like the current Artemis Accords — that lack them.
- H16.3: Generation-ship or long-duration isolation designs incorporating explicit FR (Freedom to Question) and AD (Adaptive Capacity) mechanisms from the outset will diverge from the authoritarian-lock-in trajectory documented in existing generation-ship design literature, while designs optimizing purely for mission-completion probability will tend to reproduce it.

