Chapter 20: Conditions for Theoretical Continuation

20.1 Theoretical Next Steps Only

The next stage of this research is conceptual and methodological, not empirical. No experiments will be conducted. No physical tests will be performed. No code training will be carried out.

The immediate theoretical tasks are as follows.

First, complete the literature mapping. Chapters 4 through 12 connected each principle to a primary literature, but the mapping remains incomplete. Several protective-belt claims — particularly those involving interaction effects between traits, and those involving institutional mechanisms at civilizational scale — have not yet been traced to their full evidential basis. This should be done before any empirical work.

Second, refine the tradeoff model. Chapter 13 identified five major tradeoffs: humility versus decisiveness, freedom versus convergence, empathy versus cohesion, creativity versus stability, and long-term thinking versus present need. These need to be specified more precisely. Under what conditions does each tradeoff become critical? What institutional designs manage them? What are the boundary cases?

Third, formalize the feedback loop. Chapter 14 proposed a set of state variables and failure modes, but the mapping C_{t+1}=F(C_t,E_t,I_t) is still schematic. The next step is to specify, at a purely mathematical or formal-logical level, what minimal properties F must have to generate two attractors, hysteresis, and threshold behavior. This is not simulation. It is the theoretical work that must precede simulation.

Fourth, develop the falsification protocols. Chapter 17 described adversarial collaboration and preregistration, but the protocols themselves have not been written. What would a valid adversarial test of HC1 look like? What specific measurements would settle the dispute between a defender and a critic of HC2? This can be designed entirely on paper, without collecting any data.

Fifth, clarify the boundary between the Guardian and existing constructs. Stage 3 of the original research program identified the discriminant-validity problem. The theoretical work now is to specify, for each Guardian dimension, exactly which existing construct it must outperform, and in which predictions. This can be done by argument and literature analysis, not by measurement.

These five tasks are sufficient for the next phase. They are all theoretical. They do not require experiments, physical tests, or code training.

