20.2 The Anti-Dogmatism Constraint Applied to This Draft

The theory contains a built-in safety mechanism:

\text{If } G \text{ becomes ideology, then } G \text{ violates its own principles.}

This draft must now apply that constraint to itself.

The draft is not yet an ideology. It is a research programme with a narrow hard core, a revisable protective belt, and a set of specified falsification conditions. It has not claimed to be confirmed. It has not claimed to know the correct future. It has not designated a privileged class of guardians.

But the draft could become an ideology if its authors or defenders begin to treat it as finished, as obviously true, or as immune to criticism. The warning signs would be:

· treating the ten-dimensional vector as a moral score rather than a provisional operationalization;
· defending the specific instruments or mechanisms as if they were permanent truths rather than revisable auxiliary assumptions;
· refusing to engage with critics who reject the hard core itself;
· using the language of epistemic humility to dismiss challenges to the theory;
· claiming that the theory is too important to be falsified;
· reserving to the theory's own authors the sole right to judge whether the programme has degenerated.

None of these warning signs is present in the draft as written. But the draft alone cannot prevent them from appearing later. Only the future conduct of the research programme can do that.

The correct posture is therefore not triumph. It is discipline.

---

20.3 Closing Statement

This draft has attempted to construct a theory of the human being capable of preserving and advancing civilization without claiming to know what civilization should become.

It has argued that civilization depends on more than technology and institutions. It depends on the human capacity to recognize error, to extend moral concern beyond the tribe, to question without fear, to cooperate through well-designed rules, to create without destroying stability, to think beyond a single lifetime, to hold a cosmic perspective without arrogance, to pursue technological ambition with discipline, to adapt without waiting for crisis, and to transmit knowledge without transmitting rigidity.

It has treated these capacities not as virtues to be praised, but as fragile properties to be engineered, measured, and protected.

It has deliberately made itself falsifiable. It has deliberately made itself incomplete.

It ends, as it began, with the willingness to say:

I may be wrong.

That sentence is not the weakness of the Civilization Guardian.

It is the first and last principle.



