Appendix G — Theoretical Propositions and Argument Sketches

These are not empirical proofs. They are logical clarifications intended to expose the structure of the theory.

G.1 No single trait is sufficient

Proposition P13.1: A civilization cannot achieve resilience by maximizing one Guardian trait alone.

Argument sketch:
Each trait maps to a distinct failure mode in the civilizational feedback loop. If creativity is high but cooperation is below threshold, fragmentation occurs. If cooperation is high but freedom to question is absent, rigidity occurs. Under the threshold model:

R_c \approx \min\left(\frac{EH}{EH_0}, \frac{EM}{EM_0}, \ldots, \frac{IT}{IT_0}\right) \cdot f(\ldots)

If any critical dimension is below threshold, the minimum term is near zero, regardless of how high other dimensions are. Therefore sufficiency requires multiple simultaneous thresholds.

G.2 Institutions can compensate for individual deficits

Proposition P13.3: A society of moderately humble individuals with strong adversarial institutions may outperform a society of highly humble individuals with no institutional error correction.

Argument sketch:
Civilizational Guardian capacity is an institutional property, not an individual average. Institutions can perform epistemic functions even when individual members are only moderately humble. Since:

\text{Civilizational Guardian Capacity} \neq \text{Average Individual } G

the relationship between individual trait distribution and civilizational outcomes is mediated by institutional design. Therefore individual deficit can be compensated, within limits, by institutional mechanism.

G.3 Freedom to question is a gatekeeper variable

Proposition P14.2: Declines in freedom to question should precede measurable declines in adaptive capacity.

Argument sketch:
FR enters every stage of the learning loop: information acquisition, evaluation, belief updating, and institutional adaptation. If FR degrades, all downstream stages lose capacity. Therefore FR degradation should be observable before downstream adaptive failures, making it a potential early warning indicator.

G.4 Error-detection redundancy protects the loop

Proposition P14.3: Multiple independent evaluation channels are more robust than a single channel.

Argument sketch:
If evaluation channels are sufficiently independent, the probability that all fail simultaneously is the product of their individual failure probabilities. Therefore redundancy reduces joint failure probability. A single channel is a single point of epistemic failure.

G.5 Two attractors and hysteresis

Proposition P14.1: Civilizations can persist in apparent stability while approaching a threshold beyond which recovery is impossible.

Argument sketch:
The learning and concentration trajectories are self-reinforcing. In the concentration trajectory, power concentration enables information control; information control enables dogma; dogma suppresses dissent; suppressed dissent causes information loss; information loss causes error; error erodes legitimacy; crisis produces either collapse or further concentration. Once inside this basin, the same feedbacks make exit difficult even if the original precipitating conditions disappear. Hence apparent stability is not resilience.

G.6 The anti-dogmatism constraint is internally self-referential

Proposition: If G becomes ideology, then G violates its own principles.

Argument sketch:
An ideology assigns permanent immunity from correction to its own central content. But HC2 states that no individual, group, or institution should possess permanent immunity from correction. Therefore if the Civilization Guardian framework becomes an ideology, it violates HC2 and its own first principle. This is a definitional consequence, not an empirical claim.

G.7 Agency erosion is institutional, not merely technical

Proposition from Chapter 15: A civilization can use AI extensively without losing resilience, provided contestability, responsibility, and learning are preserved.

Argument sketch:
AI agency erosion operates through three pathways: human disenfranchisement, AI enfranchisement, and AI agenda control. All three can be blocked by institutional design: preserving human judgment at critical points, human understanding of critical systems, and human responsibility for outcomes. Therefore the determining variable is institutional, not technical.

